# BUG-227 regression fixture — `tokenize` splits on ANY character in `seps`.
#
# THE SEPARATOR MUST BE MULTI-CHARACTER OR THIS FIXTURE IS VACUOUS. With a single-char
# `seps`, tokenizeSequence and tokenizeAny agree, so a test using "," would pass under the
# bug and prove nothing. Every case below uses 2+ separator characters.
#
# Was: codegen emitted std.mem.tokenizeSequence, which splits on the whole SEQUENCE, so
# `"a,b;c".tokenize(",;")` returned ONE token — the entire string — with no error at any
# stage. Documented behaviour (QUICKSTART) is "split on ANY character in seps".
#
# `split` deliberately still uses splitSequence: whole-sequence splitting is what `split`
# is FOR, and it is what makes the two methods distinct.
def main()
    var n = 0
    var joined = ""
    for t in "a,b;c".tokenize(",;")
        n = n + 1
        joined = joined + t
    assert n == 3, "tokenize splits on any separator character, not the sequence"
    assert joined == "abc", "tokens are the between-separator runs"

    # Empties are dropped (the other half of tokenize's contract).
    var m = 0
    for t in "x;;y,,z".tokenize(",;")
        m = m + 1
    assert m == 3, "adjacent separators do not produce empty tokens"

    # Separator ORDER must not matter — a sequence-splitter is order-sensitive.
    var k = 0
    for t in "p;q,r".tokenize(";,")
        k = k + 1
    assert k == 3, "separator order is irrelevant when splitting on any character"

    print("bug227: OK")
